---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="VMware Learning Platform: From Internal Tool to $1M+ SaaS • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["VMware", "VLP", "Lead Product Designer → Design Manager"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Case Study 04 // VMware Learning Platform</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>From Internal Tool to $1M+ SaaS Product</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				VMware Learning Platform (VLP) was a powerful internal tool, built
				over two decades. It had no consistent UX, an old architecture,
				and nothing that would make a customer want to buy it.
			</p>
			<p>
				I led the design work that turned it into a commercial SaaS
				product. Along the way I earned the engineering team's trust,
				started a design team, and we reached <strong>$1M+ in new ARR</strong>
				in the first year after launch.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-vlp/enrollment.png"
					alt="VMware Learning Platform interface showing cloud-based lab environment and training management"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				VLP: hands-on cloud labs in the browser, used for everything from
				sales demos to certification training.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Designer → Design Manager</h4>
				<p>
					Market &amp; user research, product transformation, team
					leadership.
				</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"
					><time>2020</time></small
				>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team &amp; scale</small>
				<h4>Cross-Functional SaaS Squad</h4>
				<p>
					1 Designer (me) → hired 1 designer, 14 Engineers, 1 PM, 2
					Solution Architects.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>$1M+ ARR // 30+ Clients</h4>
				<p>
					From internal tool to 30+ Fortune 500 customers and 100K+ lab
					demos.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: CONTEXT & THE CHALLENGE                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">context</small>
				<h2>An engineering-first team with a 20-year legacy tool</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					In 2020, VMware had an internal lab platform that had worked
					well for two decades. Engineers built it for engineers, and
					it handled complex virtual machine setups well.
				</p>
				<p>
					Leadership saw a market in cloud training and wanted it
					turned into a SaaS product. The challenge: a strong
					engineering team that was skeptical of design changes to
					workflows that already worked, and 20 years of technical
					debt.
				</p>

				<!-- Challenge Pillars Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CULTURE</small>
						<h4>No Trust Yet</h4>
						<p>
							As the first designer on the team, I had to show value
							quickly without slowing engineering down.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">PRODUCT</small>
						<h4>Commercial Pressure</h4>
						<p>
							Leadership wanted it modernized fast, to compete with
							smaller SaaS companies for enterprise training.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Legacy Before & Architecture Mapping -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-2.png"
								alt="Original VLP interface showing inconsistent legacy design patterns"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The original platform: powerful, but dense,
							inconsistent, and intimidating for buyers.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-13.png"
								alt="Information architecture and system mapping of legacy modules"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Information architecture: sorting out 20 years of
							nested features.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: RESEARCH & ECOSYSTEM DISCOVERY             -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">research</small>
				<h2>Instructors, architects, and sales</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I talked to three groups of users, and added technical deep
					dives and a look at competing SaaS products:
				</p>

				<!-- 3 Stakeholder Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ARCHITECTS</small>
						<h4>Environment Setup</h4>
						<p>
							Needed flexible multi-VM templates and fast setup,
							without the command line.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">INSTRUCTORS</small>
						<h4>Live Classroom Control</h4>
						<p>
							Needed to see students' screens live, help them, and
							track progress.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SALES TEAMS</small>
						<h4>Easy Demos</h4>
						<p>
							Needed one-click customer access in any browser, with
							no VPN or plugins.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: User Flow & Market Analysis -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-3.png"
								alt="User flow for creating a lab environment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Lab creation flow: every step of setting up a virtual
							environment.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-6.png"
								alt="Market positioning analysis and commercial SaaS opportunities"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The market: what enterprise buyers expected from
							hands-on IT labs.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Customers wanted labs in the browser, without the admin work
					of traditional VM setups.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- 3 Stakeholder Archetype Visuals -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-2-developers.svg"
								alt="Developers and student personas in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Learners — Need instant feedback from the
							console.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-3-architects.svg"
								alt="Solution architects persona in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Lab Architects — Power users building complex
							network setups.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-4-sales.svg"
								alt="Enterprise sales and field engineers persona in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Sales &amp; SEs — Running live demos in front of
							customers.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: STRATEGY & EARNING TRUST                   -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">strategy</small>
				<h2>Earning trust one sprint at a time</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					In an engineering-led team, proposing a ground-up redesign is
					the fastest way to hit a wall. So I didn't. I proposed <strong
						>small, useful design fixes</strong
					> every sprint.
				</p>
				<p>
					I fixed legibility, alignment, and usability in the features
					engineers were already building. That showed design could
					speed them up, not slow them down.
				</p>

				<!-- Strategy Callout Box -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow mb-2">EARNING TECHNICAL TRUST</small>
					<h4>Small fixes over a big redesign</h4>
					<p>
						Engineers came around when they saw that reusable
						components made front-end work faster, and that good UX
						meant less QA rework.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Sprint Tweaks & Team Alignment -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-4.png"
								alt="Iterative UI improvements and sprint-by-sprint design polish"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Sprint fixes: table density, status badges, and
							toolbars.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-5-trust.avif"
								alt="Engineering collaboration and design integration milestone"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Shared design tokens and a component library, built
							with the dev leads.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Once the team saw the value, they backed bigger changes.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: THE TURNING POINT - CLASSROOM POC          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">innovation</small>
				<h2>The Classroom Experience: the turning point</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The turning point came when I was asked to design a new <strong
						>Classroom Experience</strong
					> module from scratch. Instead of treating it as one more
					feature, I used it to show what a modern SaaS version of VLP
					could look like.
				</p>
				<p>
					I mapped the full instructor and student journey, combining
					live VM status with simple controls for the instructor:
				</p>

				<!-- Full Width Journey Map -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-11.png"
							alt="End-to-end user journey of VLP instructors and students"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						User journey: starting a session, getting students into
						labs, helping them live, and evaluating after class.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Side by Side: Instructor View & Student View -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-12.png"
								alt="Instructor classroom management interface"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Instructor view: every student's VM at a glance, with
							a way to step in.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-10.png"
								alt="Student interactive lab sandbox environment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Student view: lab instructions next to a live
							console.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					The Classroom POC showed that enterprise labs could feel as
					smooth as a modern web app.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: SOLUTION - VLP LITE & COMMERCIAL LAUNCH    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">solution</small>
				<h2>Designing VLP Lite for commercial enterprise customers</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					For the commercial market, I designed <strong>VLP Lite</strong>,
					a simpler self-service version that customers could get
					started with quickly, without losing the power
					underneath.
				</p>

				<!-- 4 Principles Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">DISCLOSURE</small>
						<h4>Progressive Complexity</h4>
						<p>
							Advanced networking settings behind sensible
							defaults, so non-technical admins could launch labs in
							minutes.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LANGUAGE</small>
						<h4>Simplified Terminology</h4>
						<p>
							Replaced internal engineering jargon with the terms
							training teams already use.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ACCESSIBILITY</small>
						<h4>Universal HTML5 Access</h4>
						<p>
							Any browser, any device. No plugins, VPN, or client
							install.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CONFIDENCE</small>
						<h4>Commercial Polish</h4>
						<p>
							A modern visual design that looked credible to
							enterprise buyers.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- 3 Highlights Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-12-browser.webp"
								alt="HTML5 in-browser lab access"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Web Access — Works on laptops and tablets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-13-modular.webp"
								alt="Modular UI architecture"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Modular — The same components for self-paced
							and live classes.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-14-console.webp"
								alt="Advanced virtual machine console controls"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Console — Fast VM console with multi-monitor
							support.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				<!-- VLP Lite Interface Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-6">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-19.png"
							alt="VLP Lite enterprise-ready interface"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						VLP Lite: launched and tested with Fortune 500 customers at
						VMworld.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: SCALING THE PLATFORM & TEAM                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">system</small>
				<h2>Redesigning the platform and growing the team</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					After VLP Lite took off, I moved into a <strong
						>Design Manager role</strong
					>, hired a product designer, and we redesigned every major
					module of the platform:
				</p>

				<!-- 6-Grid of Platform Modules -->
				<div
					class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4"
				>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-20.png"
								alt="LabBuilder multi-VM topology designer"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. LabBuilder — Set up multi-VM network
							topologies.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-16.png"
								alt="Global event management and scheduling dashboard"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Events — Schedule training sessions across
							regions.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/Dashboard_1.png"
								alt="Executive and operational lab metrics dashboard"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Dashboard — Active labs, seat usage, and host
							health.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/Mockup-2.png"
								alt="Multi-device lab mockup on modern hardware"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							04. Cross-Device — Check on labs from a tablet or
							desktop.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-1.png"
								alt="Enterprise lab catalog and enrollment portal"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							05. Lab Catalog — Find a course and start a lab in
							one click.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-17.png"
								alt="Student progress tracking and analytics"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							06. Student Analytics — Progress and completion.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: RESULTS & OUTCOME                          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article id="outcome" class="case-section border-b-0 pb-0">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h2>Results</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					VLP went from an internal tool with skeptical stakeholders to
					an official VMware commercial product.
				</p>
				<p>
					In the first year after launch it brought in $1M+ in ARR
					from 30+ Fortune 500 customers. In testing, non-technical
					admins completed 95%+ of tasks.
				</p>

				<!-- Impact Scorecard Grid: 4 Metric Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">REVENUE</small>
						<h4>$1M+ ARR</h4>
						<p>Generated in year 1 of commercial launch.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CUSTOMERS</small>
						<h4>30+ Clients</h4>
						<p>Fortune 500 enterprises onboarded.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SESSIONS</small>
						<h4>12K+ Delivered</h4>
						<p>Live global enterprise training sessions.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SCALE</small>
						<h4>100K+ Labs</h4>
						<p>Hands-on sandbox demo environments run.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Usability and Market Adoption Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 mb-8">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-18.png"
							alt="Usability test results and commercial adoption curve"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						Usability testing: 95%+ task completion among non-technical
						admins.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Key Learnings Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LEARNING 01</small>
						<h4>Trust First</h4>
						<p>
							I had to respect what engineers had already built
							before they'd listen to me about what to change.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LEARNING 02</small>
						<h4>Small Wins Add Up</h4>
						<p>
							Fixing real problems every sprint did more for trust
							than any redesign pitch would have.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LEARNING 03</small>
						<h4>Tie It to the Business</h4>
						<p>
							When design is tied to revenue and customers, it
							gets a seat at the leadership table.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Closing Reflection -->
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						"I didn't ask engineers to trust design. I showed up
						every sprint with something that made their work
						easier."
					</p>
				</blockquote>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- Page Footer Navigation -->
	<footer
		class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-black/15 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4"
	>
		<a href="/" class="btn-secondary flex items-center gap-2">
			<span>←</span>
			<span>Back to homepage</span>
		</a>
		<a
			href="#top"
			onclick="event.preventDefault(); window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });"
			class="btn-secondary flex items-center gap-1.5 cursor-pointer"
			title="Scroll to top"
		>
			<span>Top</span>
			<span>↑</span>
